\citation{gneiting2007strictly}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Is the posterior over container efficiency calibrated? Each point or bar is the fraction of cases in which the 90\% credible interval for $\theta _j$ contained the target $\theta ^\ast _j$; a calibrated posterior would sit on the dotted line at 0.90. \textbf  {(a)} Selection replicates 1000--1009, one curve per container, against the beta-binomial correlation $\rho _U$; the thick black curve is the mean over containers, which is what the selection rule maximised. \textbf  {(b)} Held-out replicates 0--29 at the selected $\rho _U{=}0.1$, per container. Five containers are covered perfectly and the near-dead one is covered in 0.20 of cases at this $\rho _U$ (0.00--0.03 across the extended grid of Table~\ref {tab:r4calib}).}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:calib}{{S1}{4}{}{}{}}
